// Unit tests for the entity store and the per-frame simulation.
//
// Every expected value is arithmetic that can be checked by hand and is written
// out in its own label, so a failure names the rule that broke rather than a
// number that moved. The units are the point of most of them: positions and
// velocities are millunits and millunits per second, gravity is millunits per
// second squared, and a frame is 1000/fps milliseconds.
//
// The store is exercised through ent_at/ent_put rather than through a scene,
// because a scene would test the loader too and this is about what a frame does
// to a record that is already there.

main(int argc, string[] argv) {
  fx_trig_init();
  ent_init();
  t_boot();
} return int 0;

// scn_init too: sim_grav reads the scene's gravity, and an export does not exist
// until its declaring function has run.
t_boot() {
  scn_init();
  t_defs();
} return void;

// A fresh record is zero everywhere except the four fields whose zero is wrong.
//
// The two ent_add calls are two statements. Written as one expression -- `"" +
// ent_add() + ent_add()` -- they print in whichever order the emitted C evaluates
// them, and this test printed "1 0" until they were split. It is the trap
// NAMES.md 6 names first, and a test is exactly as susceptible to it as anything
// else.
t_defs() {
  int ent_n_v = ent_n();
  print("ent_n on an empty store exp 0 = " + ent_n_v);
  t_defs_add();
} return void;
